The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls this one known parser issue with one fix queued.
Its own executable table separates the merged reports into two causes. They are tracked separately in
[parser issue #1](https://github.com/marcengineb/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) and
[hint issue #2](https://github.com/marcengineb/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2).
This reassessment preserves the export's historical evidence and records the course's later engineering update.
The proposed triage policy below has not been accepted by the desk.

## Engineering update: closed does not always mean fixed

Source: the [S01E03 course page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows), whose engineering update
appeared after the course accepted both submitted tickets. It reports `#4471 · closed 05 oct`.
The source gives no clock time or timezone for that closure.

| Cause | Course outcome | Closure rationale for the corresponding GitHub issue |
|---|---|---|
| `parser` | **FIXED** — `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | #1: completed, based on the reported parser fix |
| `hint` | **WON'T FIX** — `booking-v3 form retired` | #2: not planned, because the affected form was retired |

The hint bug was not reported fixed. The course describes a product retirement, not a correction to its date hint.
Both issues can therefore be closed for different reasons. Neither this update nor a closed status proves
that every historical one-day-move report was reproduced or independently resolved.
This is an outcome reported by the course; the export does not contain the production patch or regression results.

## Parser: day and month reversed

The shared parser reads dates month first even when the tenant writes day first. In the export's rule,
this covers app and email reports, plus form reports that do not use the misleading booking-v3 hint.
For example, `R-41200` describes 7 April becoming 4 July. `R-41205` belongs here despite its intake tag
of `hint`: its tenant is `morrow.tax`, locale `fr-FR`, form `booking-v2`, and its channel is `app`.

Expected fix: parse according to the tenant's explicit date contract, or pass an unambiguous machine date.
Verify both ambiguous and unambiguous dates across the affected channels. The export supplies classification
logic, not production parser code; these describe the expected behavior rather than a test result for the reported fix.

## Hint: the form tells people the wrong format

For a day-first tenant on `booking-v3`, the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, but reads the input in the tenant's order.
Following the hint therefore changes the date. This is a separate form contract problem, even if the visible
symptom resembles the parser issue. `R-41230` is a `copper.studio` form report and belongs here despite its
intake tag of `parser`.

If the form were retained, the displayed format and its interpretation would need to agree with the tenant's locale.
The actual course outcome is retirement of booking-v3, so this issue is not planned for correction.
Changing only the shared parser would not establish that this form problem was fixed.

